######################################################################
### PARLEY: THE NEGOTIATING TABLE - v2 panels, deal sheet and contextual controls
###
### A types-only file. NO registration in gui/scripted_widgets is needed
### (same reasoning as tnt_types.gui; proven by
###  MOD\3276373926\gui\00_jdb_bc_map_icon_layer.gui).
###
### WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
###   tnt_types.gui already carries the row chrome, the columns and the small
###   widgets. Everything NEW that v2 needs - the contextual "chosen object"
###   controls (user item 3.3), the centre deal sheet (item 3.4), the contextual
###   card, the prisoner panel row (defect D3's redesign - RETIRED with the
###   prisoner terms, user item 7, and commented out in section 4) and the four
###   shells reserved for build 2 - lives here so tnt_types.gui stays readable.
###
### v3 ITERATION 1 CHANGES IN THIS FILE
###   ADD 4 the deal sheet's tnt_sheet_alliance_r and tnt_sheet_truce_r rows are
###       DELETED. Each pact is one term between two rulers and is now one mutual
###       row in the centre column bound to tnt_alliance_p / tnt_truce_p; the _r
###       variables have no writer and no reader left.
###       V13 USER ITEM 1: BOTH PACTS ARE RETIRED OUTRIGHT, so the two surviving
###       _p sheet rows are commented out in section 2b as well and the mutual
###       lines in the centre column went with them. A DESIGN REFUSAL, not a bug
###       fix - vanilla gates both behind the Diplomacy perk
###       defensive_negotiations_perk. Tombstones A and B,
###       gui\tnt_diplomacy_window.gui.
###   V13 the claim groups tnt_sheet_sel_claim_p / _r are commented out in this
###       file with their types in gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui: FOUR sheet
###       groups now, not six (title x2, artifact x2). Same ruling, different
###       vanilla gate - claim fabrication is a LEARNING investment.
###   ADD 4 `block "tnt_toggle_line_icon" {}` on type tnt_toggle_line - empty by
###       default, so the seven obligation rights that already use that type are
###       untouched. The two mutual pact rows use it to keep the icon they had
###       while they lived in a column.
###   CAP the two domain counters are now the FIRST two lines of
###       tnt_context_card. With the selection cap gone they are the only signal
###       that a big gift is a bad idea, so they are read first; they already turn
###       red above the limit through tnt_domain_over_p/_r_value.
###
### BUILD 2 CHANGES IN THIS FILE
###   2.2  THE DEAL SHEET NOW LISTS MULTIPLE OBJECTS PER TERM. The six
###        single-object rows (title / claim / artifact x 2 sides) became six
###        instances of author:multiselect's group types, tnt_sheet_sel_title_p
###        and friends: a head row with the term's price plus a nested
###        `datamodel` naming every chosen object. The six deleted variables
###        tnt_title_p/_r, tnt_claim_p/_r, tnt_artifact_p/_r (30-V2-DESIGN
###        section 3.1) are no longer named anywhere in this file.
###   2.2  `type tnt_chosen_count` - the filled-state control for a multi-select
###        row: a count badge instead of one object's icon.
###   U1a  TWO DOMAIN COUNTERS in the contextual card, as instances of
###        author:multiselect's tnt_multi_domain_line. MANDATORY once several
###        titles can be ceded at once (30-V2-DESIGN section 1 row U1). The
###        card's height cap rose from 96 to 180 to hold them.
###   2.1  a marriage-pair summary line and a contract summary line in the card,
###        both through loc keys that print the variables themselves, so no
###        separator string is hard-coded in this .gui (Law R14).
###
###   TWO TYPES WERE WRITTEN IN THIS FILE AND THEN DELETED, and both deletions
###   are recorded at the site where the type used to be (sections 2a and 3b):
###   a per-object sheet sub-row and a domain counter. Both were superseded by
###   gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui, whose author owns the values, the loc keys
###   and the geometry contract those two widgets have to agree with.
###
### R-LAYOUT, obeyed in every type below (30-V2-DESIGN section 9 D2):
###   a direct child of hbox / vbox / flowcontainer carries NEITHER `position`
###   NOR `parentanchor`. Cross-axis centring is done by wrapping the child in a
###   plain `widget` and anchoring INSIDE that, which is what vanilla's own
###   stepper does (GAME\gui\window_knights.gui:716-759 - the hbox children have
###   no anchors, `parentanchor = center` appears only inside button_round at
###   :731 and :755).
###
### R-SPILL, obeyed on every text widget that prints GAME text (item 3.2c):
###   `autoresize = no` + `layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding`, and the
###   inherited `elide = right` is never overridden. type text_single inherits
###   autoresize = yes (GAME\gui\preload\labels.gui:6-16), and a self-resizing
###   child of an hbox reports a minimum width equal to its full text width, so
###   one long duchy name pushes the row's controls outside the column. The exact
###   pairing used here is vanilla's at GAME\gui\frontend_bookmarks.gui:1869-1876.
###
### THE HEIGHT CONVENTION ("floor + content + cap"), canonical statement - every
### mod panel follows one of three tiers, and a height that fits none of them is
### a defect:
###   TIER 1 - PANEL SHELLS (vbox): `size = { W 0 }` + `maximumsize = { W cap }`,
###     so the vbox measures its children and the cap preserves the old ceiling.
###     In-tree proof: tnt_panel_multi (gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui) and
###     tnt_editor_panel below; as of the height pass, tnt_panel_marriage too.
###   TIER 2 - SCROLL AREAS: `autoresizescrollarea = yes` + `size = { 0 0 }` +
###     `minimumsize = { -1 floor }` + `maximumsize = { -1 cap }` - the vanilla
###     ledger pattern (GAME\gui\window_ledger.gui:9405-9408; minimumsize with
###     -1: GAME\gui\interaction_declare_war.gui:314). The floor is N visible
###     rows, so an empty list still reads as a list.
###   TIER 3 - ROWS AND ONE-LINERS: datamodel rows stay FIXED height (vanilla
###     perf rule, GAME\gui\window_epidemics.gui:449-450 "required for
###     performance reasons"); one-line read-outs (sheet rows, counter lines,
###     context lines, heads) stay fixed + elide (R-SPILL); MOD-AUTHORED PROSE
###     that must never lose words uses text_multi + autoresize = yes +
###     max_width, and its holder gets `minimumsize = { -1 H }` instead of
###     size/maximumsize (in-tree model: the rule lines,
###     gui\tnt_panel_people.gui block "tnt_multi_domain").
###
### Every base type was verified on disk while writing this file:
###   hbox / vbox / widget / icon / textbox        GAME\gui\preload\defaults.gui
###   text_single / text_multi                     GAME\gui\preload\labels.gui:6 / :19
###   spacer / expand / divider_light              GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:590 / :597 / :944
###   button_standard                              GAME\gui\shared\buttons.gui:153
###   button_standard_list                         GAME\gui\shared\buttons.gui:348
###   button_round                                 GAME\gui\shared\buttons.gui:1149
###   button_checkbox (30x30)                      GAME\gui\shared\buttons.gui:1322
###   button_plus_small / button_minus_small        GAME\gui\shared\buttons_icons.gui:596 / :608
###   header_pattern                               GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:683
###   scrollbox                                    GAME\gui\shared\lists.gui:3185
###   button_artifact                              GAME\gui\window_inventory.gui:1606
###   coa_title_tiny / _crown                      GAME\gui\shared\coat_of_arms.gui:595 / :591
###   portrait_head_tiny                           GAME\gui\window_ledger.gui:6867
###   portrait_head_small                          GAME\gui\shared\portraits.gui:45
###   Background_Area / _Dark / _Solid             GAME\gui\shared\backgrounds.gui:41 / :51 / :69
###   Window_Background / Window_Margins           GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:144 / :122
###   Scrollbox_Margins (15/15/15/20)              GAME\gui\shared\windows.gui:135
###
### DELIBERATELY NOT DERIVED FROM scrollbox: a scan of all 373 vanilla .gui
### files finds ZERO `type X = scrollbox`. tnt_deal_sheet is therefore a vbox
### that the caller drops into a plain scrollbox's `scrollbox_content`, which
### takes exactly one child (GAME\gui\shared\lists.gui:3182).
######################################################################

types TwdPanels
{

	####################################################################
	### 1. THE CONTEXTUAL "CHOSEN OBJECT" CONTROL   (user item 3.3)
	###
	### The complaint was: "the artifact and non-aggression rows use generic
	### buttons that do not fit their content". The answer is a two-state
	### control slot on tnt_item_row_picker: while nothing is chosen the row
	### keeps the ordinary `button_standard "tnt_btn_pick"`; once something IS
	### chosen that button hides and a CONTENT-SHAPED control appears -
	### the real artifact icon with its rarity frame, the third party's
	### portrait, the title's coat of arms.
	###
	### The two states are mutually exclusive `visible =` expressions on the
	### already-shipped guard .Artifact.IsValid / .Char.IsValid / .Title.IsValid
	### (vanilla-proven form: GAME\gui\window_government_administration.gui:3233
	### `Not( Title.MakeScope.Var('...').Title.IsValid )`; already used in this
	### mod at tnt_diplomacy_window.gui's tnt_picker_label blockoverrides).
	###
	### The wrapper is a button_standard holding an `alwaystransparent = yes`
	### widget, which is EXACTLY vanilla's own artifact row:
	###   GAME\gui\interaction_templates.gui:517-533
	###     button_standard = { ... hbox = { widget = { size = { 80 80 }
	###       alwaystransparent = yes  button_artifact = { size = { 100% 100% } } }
	### alwaystransparent on the holder is what makes the whole subtree
	### click-through, so the OUTER button owns the click and the inner
	### button_artifact / coa / portrait never swallows it.
	###
	### 10+ vanilla types derive from button_standard, e.g.
	### GAME\gui\window_ledger.gui:3860 `type button_basic_ledger_item`.
	####################################################################
	### `size = { 44 100% }`, i.e. the FULL row height, is deliberate. R-LAYOUT
	### forbids a parentanchor on this widget because its parent is the control
	### hbox, so a 44x44 button would be laid out at that hbox's cross-axis origin
	### instead of centred. Filling the height and centring the CONTENT inside is
	### the same solution vanilla uses for its own artifact row, where the wrapper
	### button_standard is `size = { 100% 100% }` of an 80 px item
	### (GAME\gui\interaction_templates.gui:513-533).
	type tnt_chosen_button = button_standard
	{
		name = "tnt_chosen_button"
		size = { 44 100% }
		using = tooltip_es

		### Hidden by default: the EMPTY state is the normal case, and a row
		### that has never been touched must not flash a control.
		block "tnt_chosen_visible"
		{
			visible = no
		}

		### The instantiation site supplies datacontext = "[TntVar('tnt_x').Artifact]"
		### (or .Char / .Title). Kept as a block so the type stays item-agnostic.
		block "tnt_chosen_context" {}

		### Clicking the chosen object re-opens its picker: same scripted gui as
		### the empty state's button, so there is exactly one entry point per row.
		block "tnt_chosen_click" {}

		block "tnt_chosen_tooltip" {}

		widget = {
			name = "tnt_chosen_holder"
			size = { 100% 100% }
			alwaystransparent = yes

			block "tnt_chosen_content" {}
		}
	}


	### ================================================================
	### BUILD 3 - tnt_chosen_artifact AND tnt_chosen_title ARE SUPERSEDED,
	### AND THAT IS THE ANSWER TO ITEM 3.3, NOT AN OMISSION.
	### ================================================================
	### Both are written, both are correct, and both have ZERO instantiations. All
	### six title / claim / artifact rows use tnt_chosen_count instead
	### (tnt_diplomacy_window.gui:584, :620, :813, :1177, :1212, :1332), and the long
	### note above that type says why in one line: "there is no single object to draw
	### once a term can hold five titles". Item 3.3 asked for a content-shaped
	### control per row; for a multi-select term the content IS a count, so the count
	### badge is the content-shaped control. The one row that still holds exactly one
	### object - promise not to attack a third party - does get its shaped control,
	### tnt_chosen_char (:851, :1365).
	### THEY ARE KEPT because the moment any of the six rows is capped at one object
	### again, or an "exactly one selected" state wants the real icon beside the
	### count, this is the finished widget for it - `tnt_chosen_button` + one
	### blockoverride, no new geometry. An uninstantiated type costs nothing at load.
	### If a later author instantiates one, note that tnt_chosen_title needs a Title
	### datacontext and tnt_chosen_artifact an Artifact one, supplied by the INSTANCE
	### through block "tnt_chosen_context" - the count badge needs neither, which is
	### the other reason it suited six rows that read a script value.
	###
	### ARTIFACT: the real icon plus vanilla's rarity backing frame.
	### `blockoverride "on_click" {}` strips button_artifact's own two onclicks
	### (ToggleGameViewData('artifact_details', ...) and
	###  LocalPlayerNewArtifacts.RemoveNewArtifact) - GAME\gui\window_inventory.gui:1640-1644.
	### Neither belongs in a negotiation, and the view-opening family of keys is
	### blacklisted (00-ARCHITECTURE section 4.6 + 30-V2-DESIGN R4). None of those
	### blacklisted names is SPELLED anywhere in this mod, so a mechanical
	### blacklist grep stays at zero hits.
	type tnt_chosen_artifact = tnt_chosen_button
	{
		blockoverride "tnt_chosen_content"
		{
			button_artifact = {
				name = "tnt_chosen_artifact_icon"
				size = { 38 38 }
				parentanchor = center

				blockoverride "on_click" {}
			}
		}
	}


	### THIRD PARTY (promise not to attack): the character's own head.
	### 38 px matches the size this mod already ships for portrait_head_tiny in
	### tnt_picker_row_char, so the crop is the one already seen in game.
	type tnt_chosen_char = tnt_chosen_button
	{
		blockoverride "tnt_chosen_content"
		{
			portrait_head_tiny = {
				name = "tnt_chosen_char_portrait"
				size = { 38 38 }
				parentanchor = center
				### USER ITEM 17(c): the only portrait living in the table besides
				### the two column heads (the nonwar rows instantiate this type).
				### Portraits render in a later pass than flat widgets and punch
				### through the modal dims, so it hides while a picker or a preset
				### menu is up - same gate and same vanilla call sites as
				### tnt_column_portrait, gui\tnt_types.gui (Or/Not:
				### GAME\gui\frontend_main.gui:891; GetVariableSystem.Exists:
				### GAME\gui\hud.gui:1006).
				visible = "[Not( Or( TntOn('tnt_picker'), GetVariableSystem.Exists( 'tnt_preset_menu' ) ) )]"
			}
		}
	}


	### TITLE / CLAIM: the title's coat of arms.
	###
	### DEVIATION FROM 30-V2-DESIGN, stated on purpose: the design names
	### coa_title_tiny_crown. That variant re-shows the `crown` icon, and the
	### crown strip is 22 px tall on top of the 36 px CoA with spacing -4
	### (GAME\gui\shared\coat_of_arms.gui:595-655), i.e. 54 px - taller than a
	### 56 px row can hold with any padding at all. Plain coa_title_tiny leaves
	### the crown hidden and, because its flowcontainer is `ignoreinvisible = yes`,
	### the hidden crown costs no height: the control is 36 px.
	### `blockoverride "position"` is required because coa_title_tiny shifts its
	### flowcontainer up by 10 px to make room for that crown (block "position" at
	### :601-603); with the crown hidden the shift only pushes the CoA out of the
	### button. That override is VERBATIM VANILLA on this very type -
	### GAME\gui\window_county_view.gui:3561-3565:
	###     coa_title_tiny = {
	###         datacontext = "[HoldingView.GetHolder.GetPrimaryTitle]"
	###         blockoverride "position" {
	###             position = { 0 0 }
	###         }
	###     }
	### (also GAME\gui\window_travel_route_edit.gui:242-246, empty-brace form).
	### The Title comes from the datacontext the INSTANCE puts on the outer button
	### through block "tnt_chosen_context"; datacontexts propagate down, which is
	### how the two negotiation columns already work.
	### The tier is not lost - the row's grey second line prints the full title
	### name, and the CoA's own LandedTitleTooltip is left intact.
	type tnt_chosen_title = tnt_chosen_button
	{
		blockoverride "tnt_chosen_content"
		{
			coa_title_tiny = {
				name = "tnt_chosen_title_coa"
				parentanchor = center

				blockoverride "position"
				{
					position = { 0 0 }
				}
			}
		}
	}


	### MULTI-SELECT (item 2.2): a count badge.
	###
	### There is no single object to draw once a term can hold five titles, so the
	### filled state of a multi-select row shows HOW MANY are on the table and
	### re-opens the panel on click - the same one entry point per row the
	### single-object controls above use.
	###
	### The digit itself comes from author:multiselect's
	### tnt_count_sel_<item>_<side>_value, read through the shipped TntSV. An
	### earlier draft counted the list in the GUI instead, with
	### GetDataModelSize over MakeScope.GetList - which is vanilla and returns
	### int32 (GAME\gui\frontend_bookmarks.gui:1679 compares it,
	### GAME\gui\map_icon_layer.gui:1414 prints it) but has only ever run on
	### ENGINE datamodels, and which would have been a SECOND way to answer a
	### question the mod already answers in script. The count that prices the
	### term is the count the badge shows.
	###
	### The badge keeps autoresize at its inherited `yes` ON PURPOSE - unlike
	### every text in this file that prints game text. Its content is one or two
	### digits, its parent is the plain widget `tnt_chosen_holder` (not a layout),
	### and `parentanchor = center` needs the widget to size to its text to sit in
	### the middle of the 44 px button. R-SPILL is about layout children whose
	### full text width becomes the row's minimum width; that cannot happen here.
	type tnt_chosen_count = tnt_chosen_button
	{
		blockoverride "tnt_chosen_content"
		{
			text_single = {
				name = "tnt_chosen_count_text"
				parentanchor = center
				align = nobaseline|center
				default_format = "#high"
				alwaystransparent = yes
				fontsize_min = 12
				using = Font_Size_Medium

				block "tnt_chosen_count_label" {}
			}
		}
	}


	####################################################################
	### 2. THE DEAL SHEET   (user item 3.4 - "in the centre, in full")
	###
	### One static row per term per side, each with its own `visible`, all under
	### `ignoreinvisible = yes` so hidden rows collapse to nothing. NOT a
	### datamodel: a datamodel needs one uniform element type and our terms are
	### numbers, flags, titles, artifacts and characters. A variable-driven
	### `visible` costs one map lookup per frame (00-ARCHITECTURE section 3.4).
	###
	### Every row is FIXED SIZE - PDX's own rule, "This is required for
	### performance reasons", GAME\gui\window_epidemics.gui:449-450.
	###
	### The points column reads the item's own tnt_val_<item>_<side>_value, the
	### same value tnt_gain_total_value / tnt_loss_total_value are built from, so
	### the sheet and the breakdown tooltip can never disagree.
	###
	### ONE FAMILY OF ROWS IS THE EXCEPTION, and it does not weaken that rule: the
	### marriage pair rows print a VARIABLE stored on the pair (tnt_wed_net),
	### because those same stored numbers are what tnt_val_marriage_p/_r_value sum.
	### Reading a summand instead of the sum is still reading ONE number, not
	### deriving a second one. See the long note above tnt_sheet_marriage_pairs.
	####################################################################
	type tnt_sheet_group = hbox
	{
		name = "tnt_sheet_group"
		layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
		size = { 0 24 }
		maximumsize = { -1 24 }
		spacing = 6
		### The 3 px VERTICAL margin is what centres the text line. R-LAYOUT
		### forbids `parentanchor = vcenter` on a child of this hbox, and an hbox
		### lays its children out at the cross-axis origin, so the margin has to do
		### the job: 24 - 2*3 = 18 px of content for an 18 px line of Font_Size_Small.
		margin = { 4 3 }
		alwaystransparent = yes

		text_single = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_group_text"
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
			autoresize = no
			align = nobaseline|left
			default_format = "#high"
			alwaystransparent = yes
			fontsize_min = 12
			using = Font_Type_Flavor
			using = Font_Size_Medium

			block "tnt_sheet_group_label"
			{
				text = "tnt_sheet_you_give"
			}
		}
	}


	### Row anatomy, 26 px: [icon 20] [name 118] [detail, elided] [X 24] [points 52]
	### Width budget in the 470-wide centre panel:
	###   470 - 20 (panel margin) - 8 - 26 (scroll margins) = 416
	###   416 - 8 (row margin) = 408 = 20 + 6 + 118 + 6 + 170 + 6 + 24 + 6 + 52
	### V5 (user item 15a): the 24 px X cell took its 30 px (cell + spacing) out
	### of the elidable detail column, 200 -> 170, so every FIXED column keeps
	### its old edge.
	type tnt_sheet_row = hbox
	{
		name = "tnt_sheet_row"
		layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
		size = { 0 26 }
		maximumsize = { -1 26 }
		spacing = 6
		### 3 px vertical margin centres the line - see tnt_sheet_group above.
		margin = { 4 3 }
		### V5 (user item 15a): was alwaystransparent = yes while the row was a
		### pure read-out. The remove button below has to RECEIVE clicks, and an
		### alwaystransparent ANCESTOR makes its whole subtree click-through
		### (this mod's proven doctrine) - so the row went opaque to the hit
		### test, exactly like tnt_toggle_line, this file's other interactive
		### hbox. Every non-interactive child keeps its own alwaystransparent =
		### yes, so nothing else about hover changed.
		alwaystransparent = no
		ignoreinvisible = yes

		### THE ROW'S OWN TOOLTIP SLOT (V26). This file's doctrine for an elided
		### label is written twice - 2b below and tnt_panel_contract.gui's
		### obligation rows - as "the full name lives in the tooltip", but the
		### type never implemented it: it had no tooltip property, no block for
		### one and no using = tooltip_es, so whatever the 170 px detail column
		### cut off was simply GONE and the player had no hover to recover it.
		###
		### The block is EMPTY by default and that is what makes this safe for
		### the other thirty-odd instances: a widget carrying `using =
		### tooltip_es` and no `tooltip` property emits nothing at all - the
		### template sets only tooltip_type and the four anchors
		### (GAME\gui\preload\defaults.gui:12-19) - so a row that does not fill
		### the block is unchanged, byte for byte, in behaviour. The row is
		### already alwaystransparent = no (see just above), so no new hit-test
		### plumbing is needed, and the X button in tnt_sheet_x_holder keeps its
		### own tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip" and still wins on its own rect.
		### Only rows whose detail is PROSE fill the block; a row whose detail is
		### a number or an object name has nothing a tooltip could add.
		using = tooltip_es

		block "tnt_sheet_tooltip" {}

		### Hidden until the term is actually part of the offer. Overridden per
		### instance with TntOn('tnt_gold_p') for flags and numbers, or with
		### TntVar('tnt_title_p').Title.IsValid for object-valued terms.
		block "tnt_sheet_visible"
		{
			visible = no
		}

		### R-LAYOUT: the icon is wrapped in a plain widget so it may centre.
		widget = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_icon_holder"
			size = { 20 100% }
			alwaystransparent = yes

			icon = {
				name = "tnt_sheet_icon"
				size = { 18 18 }
				parentanchor = center
				alwaystransparent = yes

				block "tnt_sheet_icon"
				{
					texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_gold.dds"
				}
			}
		}

		### The term's name. A fixed loc key, so autoresize is harmless - but it
		### is switched off anyway so a long translation cannot widen the row.
		text_single = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_name"
			min_width = 118
			max_width = 118
			autoresize = no
			align = nobaseline|left
			default_format = "#medium"
			alwaystransparent = yes
			fontsize_min = 12
			using = Font_Size_Small

			block "tnt_sheet_name"
			{
				text = "tnt_item_gold"
			}
		}

		### The detail: the amount for a currency, the object's name for a title,
		### artifact or character. THIS is the one that prints game text, so
		### R-SPILL applies in full.
		###
		### IT IS ALSO THE ROW'S ONLY EXPANDING CHILD, which is why the eight
		### flag-only rows (alliance / marriage / hook / vassal, both sides)
		### override it with `text = ""` and NEVER with `visible = no`. This hbox
		### carries `ignoreinvisible = yes`, so an invisible child costs no space
		### at all: hiding it would collapse the 170 px of slack (200 before the
		### V5 X cell) and pull tnt_sheet_points ~170 px left, so those eight
		### rows would print their points in the middle of the row while the
		### rest print them at the right edge. `text = ""` keeps the widget in
		### the layout and empty.
		### An empty string is vanilla-proven (GAME\gui\interaction_templates.gui:1321,
		### GAME\gui\window_ledger.gui:6428).
		text_single = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_detail"
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
			autoresize = no
			align = nobaseline|left
			default_format = "#weak"
			alwaystransparent = yes
			fontsize_min = 12
			using = Font_Size_Small

			block "tnt_sheet_detail"
			{
				text = "[TntNum('tnt_gold_p')|0]"
			}
		}

		### V5 (user item 15a) - THE REMOVE CONTROL'S SLOT, a fixed 24 px cell.
		### EMPTY by default, so a row that cannot be removed inline (the derived
		### taper row, the AI's own usehook demand, the two hook-tier rows) keeps
		### its geometry by simply not filling the block. Instances fill it with
		### a 20 px button_cancel_small - the bare small X vanilla draws inline
		### with its own onclick and tooltip (GAME\gui\shared\buttons_icons.gui:50,
		### GAME\gui\window_army.gui:1111-1115).
		###
		### It sits BEFORE the points column ON PURPOSE. The two row families
		### are siblings in one scrollbox: their points columns only stay
		### flush because points is the LAST child of both, so the 30 px this
		### cell costs comes out of the elidable detail column - the one
		### width the families were never required to agree on (see 2a below).
		### A cell AFTER points would have shifted this family's numbers 30 px
		### left of the other's. V6 (user item 7): tnt_multi_sheet_row
		### (gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui) grew the SAME cell in the same
		### place, so the reasoning now binds both families identically.
		###
		### R-LAYOUT: a plain widget, so the instance's button may anchor inside
		### it; the holder itself carries no anchor. Deliberately NOT
		### alwaystransparent - a transparent ancestor would kill the X's click
		### (the row-level note above).
		widget = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_x_holder"
			size = { 24 100% }

			block "tnt_sheet_remove" {}
		}

		text_single = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_points"
			min_width = 52
			max_width = 52
			align = nobaseline|right
			default_format = "#high"
			alwaystransparent = yes
			fontsize_min = 12
			using = Font_Size_Small

			block "tnt_sheet_points"
			{
				text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_gold_p_value')|0]"
			}
		}
	}


	####################################################################
	### 2a. THERE IS NO PER-OBJECT SUB-ROW TYPE IN THIS FILE. It was written and
	### then deleted, and the note stays so nobody writes it a second time.
	###
	### The multi-select sheet groups are `tnt_sheet_sel_title_p` and its siblings,
	### from gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui (six until V13 user item 1 retired the
	### claim pair; FOUR live now), and the per-object row inside
	### them is that file's `tnt_multi_sheet_row`. Two 20-something-pixel row
	### families living as siblings in ONE scrollbox is a real defect, not a
	### duplication smell: the sheet's columns only line up while every row family
	### agrees on height, spacing, margin, name width and points width, and
	### author:multiselect's header states that its geometry is copied from
	### tnt_sheet_row below TO THE PIXEL. One copy can keep that promise; two
	### cannot.
	###
	### One architectural limit is worth restating here because it explains why the
	### per-object rows print NO points. A per-object price would need a script
	### value evaluated with the title or artifact as a scope, and this mod can
	### never do that: GuiScope.SetRoot(X).GetScriptValueBreakdown and ScriptValue
	### supply ROOT ONLY (00-ARCHITECTURE section 2.7, "Hard limit"), Law R9
	### restricts every balance value to root plus var: reads, and Law R8 forbids
	### chaining a second .AddScope. Each group's HEAD row therefore carries the
	### whole term's price, which is also the number that enters the balance, so
	### the sheet and the breakdown tooltip still cannot disagree - which is the
	### property user item 1.2 is about.
	###
	### V5 (user item 15a): tnt_sheet_row grew a 24 px remove cell BETWEEN detail
	### and points. V6 (user item 7): tnt_multi_sheet_row grew the SAME cell in
	### the same place, and its six group HEAD rows now carry the X, wired to
	### the gate-free tnt_sel_<item>_<side>_clear entries
	### (tnt_22b_multiselect.txt) - the per-object rows stay X-less because a
	### per-object removal sgui does not exist; per-object removal still lives
	### in the picker panels' chosen columns (tnt_sel_*_remove). The alignment
	### promise above survives in both families for the same reason: the cell
	### is paid for by the elidable detail column and points stays the LAST
	### child of both.
	####################################################################


	####################################################################
	### 2b. THE SHEET ITSELF - 2 group headers and one row per term per side
	### (item 19 added four rows per side: independence, hostage, ward, courtier -
	### the ward pair is RETIRED, V16 user item 5, so three per side survive).
	### USER ITEMS 1 AND 7 REMOVED FIVE ROWS: nonwar x2, prisoners x2 and the
	### picked-captives row, and V16 USER ITEM 5 REMOVED TWO MORE: ward x2. All
	### seven are commented out in place below rather than deleted.
	###
	### Dropped by the caller into a scrollbox's `scrollbox_content`, which takes
	### exactly ONE child (GAME\gui\shared\lists.gui:3182).
	###
	### EVERY instance overrides `name`. type tnt_sheet_row sets
	### name = "tnt_sheet_row" in its own body, and 30 same-named direct siblings
	### make the engine log "Duplicate children with the same name override
	### previous widgets" 30 times - and that line would contain tnt_, which the
	### acceptance checklist forbids in error.log.
	###
	### Order matches the two negotiation columns top to bottom, so the eye can
	### track a term from the column it was set in to the sheet.
	###
	### BUILD 2 hangs the six multi-select datamodels off block "tnt_sheet_multi"
	### at the bottom of each side, next to that side's single-object rows.
	###
	### V5 (user item 15a) - THE ROW X. Every removable row fills
	### block "tnt_sheet_remove" with the 20 px button_cancel_small, wired
	### STRICTLY to a scripted_gui that already exists - this file invents no
	### removal logic, so the balance re-reads by itself exactly as it does
	### when the same sgui is run from its own column control:
	###   * the four currencies x2 sides -> their _none presets
	###     (tnt_22_v2.txt), the same effect the columns' "none" preset runs;
	###   * a marriage pair -> tnt_wed_remove (tnt_22a_marriage.txt, written as
	###     "the small round X on a pair row"); the singular character rows ->
	###     tnt_pick_<item>_<side>_clear (tnt_21_pickers.txt); the list rows ->
	###     tnt_sel_<item>_<side>_clear (tnt_22b_multiselect.txt).
	###     (nonwar x2 -> tnt_nonwar_*_clear and the picked captives ->
	###      tnt_sel_prisoner_p_clear were the worked examples here until user
	###      items 1 and 7 retired those rows; the rule they illustrate stands.)
	###   * the flag terms -> their own toggles: a row is only visible while
	###     its term is ON, so Execute always lands in the remove branch.
	###     FIVE of those toggles carry an availability is_valid (alliance,
	###     truce, threat, vassal x2, usehook_p) - THREE since V13 user item 1
	###     retired the alliance and the truce with their _off wrappers; the
	###     survivors are threat, vassal x2 and usehook_p. The doctrine at
	###     tnt_22c_hooks_contracts.txt:111 - taking a term OFF the table must
	###     never be refused - wants gate-free _off wrappers instead; they do
	###     not exist yet, and this wave may not add scripted_guis. When the
	###     sgui owner ships them, rebind those Xes and delete this sentence.
	### THE ROWS WITH NO X, each for a reason recorded at the row: the taper (a
	### derived discount, not a term), the AI's usehook demand (read-only by
	### design), and - as of item 19 - the two GRANT INDEPENDENCE rows, whose
	### only registered scripted gui is the gated tnt_indep_<side>_toggle. The
	### rule is the same one the V5 note above states: an X may only be wired to
	### a gate-free entry point, because a removal must never be refusable.
	### (Two rows named here in V5 have since been wired and are no longer in
	### this list: the two hook tiers got tnt_hook_<side>_clear, and the grand
	### wedding got tnt_wed_grand_clear.)
	### The multi-select groups draw their own rows in author:multiselect's file -
	### see 2a above for where their X belongs. (Six until V13 user item 1 retired
	### the claim pair; four live now.)
	####################################################################
	type tnt_deal_sheet = vbox
	{
		name = "tnt_deal_sheet"
		layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
		spacing = 2
		ignoreinvisible = yes

		################################################################
		### WHAT YOU GIVE  (_p)
		################################################################
		tnt_sheet_group = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_head_p"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_group_label" { text = "tnt_sheet_you_give" }
		}

		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_gold_p"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_gold_p')]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_gold.dds" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_gold" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "[TntNum('tnt_gold_p')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_gold_p_value')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
			{
				button_cancel_small = {
					size = { 20 20 }
					parentanchor = center
					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_gold_p_none')]"
				}
			}
		}

		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_prestige_p"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_prestige_p')]" }
			### USER ITEM 3: was flat_icons/prestige.dds, a BLACK ALPHA MASK (every
			### opaque pixel RGB 0,0,0) - the path was always valid, the texture is
			### simply not a coloured icon. The level-texture datafunction returns
			### the coloured one: GAME\gui\hud.gui:6521-6525. GetPlayer and NOT
			### Character - a deal-sheet row has no Character in its datacontext.
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "[GetPlayer.GetPrestigeLevelTexture]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_prestige" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "[TntNum('tnt_prestige_p')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_prestige_p_value')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
			{
				button_cancel_small = {
					size = { 20 20 }
					parentanchor = center
					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_prestige_p_none')]"
				}
			}
		}

		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_piety_p"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_piety_p')]" }
			### USER ITEM 3: same black-alpha-mask defect and same cure as the
			### prestige row - GAME\gui\hud.gui:6586-6590.
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "[GetPlayer.GetPietyLevelTexture]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_piety" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "[TntNum('tnt_piety_p')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_piety_p_value')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
			{
				button_cancel_small = {
					size = { 20 20 }
					parentanchor = center
					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_piety_p_none')]"
				}
			}
		}

		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_influence_p"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_influence_p')]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_influence_01.dds" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_influence" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "[TntNum('tnt_influence_p')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_influence_p_value')|0]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
			{
				button_cancel_small = {
					size = { 20 20 }
					parentanchor = center
					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_influence_p_none')]"
				}
			}
		}

		####################################################################
		### RETIRED WITH THE ALLIANCE AND THE NON-AGGRESSION TRUCE - V13 USER ITEM 1.
		### Both pacts were built, shipped and played, then CONSIDERED AND WITHDRAWN
		### FOR AN INDEFINITE PERIOD on the user's ruling. THIS IS A DESIGN REFUSAL,
		### NOT A BUG FIX: vanilla gates both behind the same Diplomacy lifestyle
		### perk, defensive_negotiations_perk
		### (GAME\common\character_interactions\00_alliance.txt:1184, and
		### GAME\common\scripted_triggers\03_fp2_scripted_triggers.txt:725-735 whose
		### OR opens with `always = no`), so buying them at this table
		### short-circuits an investment the player never made.
		### Nothing is deleted; do not re-enable one half - each row's X calls a
		### scripted_gui (tnt_alliance_p_off / tnt_truce_p_off) that is retired with
		### it. THE TRUCE PRICE IS RETIRED; THE ALLIANCE PRICE IS NOT, and since
		### 2026-08-24 it has a live DISPLAY row at the foot of this side - a
		### consequence of the couples, never this bought term. tnt_label_years STAYS
		### DEFINED on purpose: an undefined key prints raw. INDICES 3 AND 4: retired.
		### Full tombstones: TOMBSTONE A and TOMBSTONE B on the two centre lines in
		### gui\tnt_diplomacy_window.gui, and common\scripted_effects\tnt_32_apply.txt.
		####################################################################
#		tnt_sheet_row = {
#			name = "tnt_sheet_alliance_p"
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_alliance_p')]" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/alliance.dds" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_alliance" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_alliance_p_value')|0]" }
#			### The same toggle the mutual row's checkbox executes; ON here, so
#			### it removes. Gated - see the 2b header's rebind note.
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
#			{
#				button_cancel_small = {
#					size = { 20 20 }
#					parentanchor = center
#					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
#					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_alliance_p_off')]"
#				}
#			}
#		}
#
#		tnt_sheet_row = {
#			name = "tnt_sheet_truce_p"
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_truce_p')]" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/negotiate_settlement_interaction.dds" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_truce" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "tnt_label_years" }
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_val_truce_p_value')|0]" }
#			### Same as the alliance X above: the mutual row's own gated toggle.
#			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
#			{
#				button_cancel_small = {
#					size = { 20 20 }
#					parentanchor = center
#					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
#					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_truce_p_off')]"
#				}
#			}
#		}

		### ITEM 2.1, REWRITTEN FOR V3 ITERATION 4: THE MARRIAGE TERM IS A LIST.
		###
		### The deal used to hold ONE couple, so this was one row naming
		### tnt_spouse_p. It now holds any number of couples: tnt_wed_list on the
		### player, one member per pair, each member being the PLAYER-SIDE spouse
		### and carrying the other half as tnt_wed_partner on itself.
		###
		### V4 REQUEST 4 DELETED THE HEAD ROW, AND ITS TWIN UNDER "THEY GIVE" WITH
		### IT. The two of them printed "Marriage pact / couples in this treaty: 1"
		### on each side, which reads exactly as the complaint put it - "you give one
		### character in marriage", "the partner gives one character in marriage" -
		### a single agreed COUPLE stated twice as two anonymous halves. A chosen
		### pair must appear in the terms AS A PAIR, so a couple is now ONE row and
		### that row carries its own price.
		###
		### A PER-PAIR PRICE IS POSSIBLE NOW AND WAS NOT BEFORE. Section 2a's
		### objection is unchanged and still true - GetScriptValueBreakdown and
		### ScriptValue supply ROOT ONLY, so no script value can be evaluated with a
		### pair as its scope - but the price no longer has to be evaluated here.
		### tnt_wed_add_pair_effect stores it ON the pair (tnt_wed_gain,
		### tnt_wed_loss, tnt_wed_net) at the moment the couple is added, and
		### tnt_wed_reprice_all_effect refreshes all three after every edit to the
		### table (tnt_34_marriage.txt). This row reads a VARIABLE, which needs no
		### root, and tnt_val_marriage_p/_r_value sum those very same stored numbers
		### - so the sheet still cannot disagree with the breakdown tooltip, which is
		### what item 1.2 actually asked for.
		###
		### WHICH COLUMN HOLDS WHAT, and not the other way round: the name column is
		### 118 px and fixed (:408-409), so it takes the LINE - one short concept
		### keyword; the detail column is the row's only expanding child (:427-436),
		### so it takes BOTH NAMES; the points column takes tnt_wed_net.
		###
		### THE COUNT is not lost with the head row: it moved to the detail of the
		### volume-discount row below, which is the one place where the number of
		### couples has a consequence worth printing.

		### ONE ROW PER AGREED COUPLE.
		###
		### `datacontext = "[Scope.Char]"` unwraps the list member exactly as
		### GAME\gui\window_situation_list.gui:1079 does inside this same kind of
		### datamodel, and `size = { 0 26 }` is on the ITEM because PDX require a
		### fixed size on every datamodel child ("This is required for performance
		### reasons", GAME\gui\window_epidemics.gui:449-450) - the type sets it too,
		### and the multi-select groups restate it on the item for the same reason.
		###
		### The name column holds the LINE for that couple (tnt_wed_row_line reads
		### tnt_wed_matri off the row's own character) and the detail column holds
		### both names (tnt_wed_pair_row reads tnt_wed_partner off it).
		###
		### THE POINTS COLUMN IS NO LONGER EMPTY (V4 request 4). It reads
		### tnt_wed_net off the row's own character through the same
		### `Character.MakeScope.Var( … ).GetValue` path the two loc keys above
		### already use on this very datacontext, so it needs nothing this row did
		### not already have. `|+=0` prints the sign, which a net that can be
		### negative needs - a bad match is a row that costs points and must look
		### like one (the formatter is this mod's own convention,
		### tnt_panel_contract.gui:1691, tnt_types.gui:1563).
		###
		### The number is a SNAPSHOT taken by tnt_wed_add_pair_effect and refreshed
		### by tnt_wed_reprice_all_effect, never derived here - see the note above
		### this vbox for why that is the only way a per-pair price can exist at all.
		vbox = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_marriage_pairs"
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
			spacing = 2
			datamodel = "[TntList('tnt_wed_list')]"

			item = {
				tnt_sheet_row = {
					name = "tnt_sheet_marriage_pair"
					datacontext = "[Scope.Char]"
					size = { 0 26 }

					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[TntOn('tnt_marriage_p')]" }
					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_marriage.dds" }
					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_wed_row_line" }
					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "tnt_wed_pair_row" }
					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[Character.MakeScope.Var('tnt_wed_net').GetValue|+=0]" }
					### The one PER-ELEMENT X in the sheet, because the row IS the
					### element and tnt_wed_remove's own header calls it "the small
					### round X on a pair row" - the sgui was written for this
					### click. TntPickChar hands it this row's Character (the
					### datacontext above) as scope:tnt_picked, the same call the
					### marriage panel's pair list already makes; gate-free, and
					### tnt_wed_drop_pair_effect reprices everything itself.
					blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
					{
						button_cancel_small = {
							size = { 20 20 }
							parentanchor = center
							tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
							onclick = "[TntPickChar('tnt_wed_remove')]"
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}

		### THE GRAND-WEDDING PROMISE, on the giving side because the HOST pays.
		###
		### It is one promise per deal, not one per couple - vanilla allows one
		### outstanding promise per host - so it is one row, and its detail names
		### the honoured couple by READING tnt_wed_principal (tnt_wed_grand_couple),
		### never by position: a CK3 variable list has no index access, so "the
		### first pair" is not a thing this sheet could express even if it wanted to.
		###
		### THE VOLUME DISCOUNT, and it exists BECAUSE the pair rows now carry their
		### own prices. Each pair's stored gain is what that match is worth ON ITS
		### OWN; tnt_val_marriage_p_value then multiplies the SUM of the gains by
		### tnt_marriage_taper_value (tnt_52_marriage_values.txt:2086-2122, x0.95 at two
		### pairs down to x0.80 at five). So from the second couple onwards
		### the rows above would add up to slightly more than the balance moves, and
		### an unexplained gap between the sheet and the scale is the exact class of
		### bug this project keeps being told not to ship.
		###
		### The row makes the gap a TERM. Its number is
		### tapered_sum - untapered_sum, which is <= 0 by construction, so
		###     sum of pair nets  +  this row  +  the grand promise below
		###     ==  tnt_val_marriage_p_value - tnt_val_marriage_r_value
		### exactly, and the arithmetic a player can do by eye now closes.
		###
		### IT IS HIDDEN AT ONE COUPLE, where the taper is x1.00 and the row would
		### print a truthful but pointless "0" (the script value returns 0 there, and
		### `visible` tests the list size for the same reason - a row that says
		### nothing is a row that costs 26 px of the centre column, which is the
		### thing V4 iteration 3 was spent shortening).
		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_wed_taper"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[And( TntOn('tnt_marriage_p'), GreaterThanOrEqualTo_int32( GetDataModelSize( TntList('tnt_wed_list') ), '(int32)2' ) )]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_marriage.dds" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_wed_taper" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "tnt_wed_taper_detail" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_marriage_taper_cut_value')|+=0]" }
			### NO X (user item 15a): a derived discount, not a term - it is
			### removed by removing pairs, so block "tnt_sheet_remove" stays empty.
		}

		### POINTS OF ITS OWN, AS OF V4. The +40 vanilla pays for a promised grand
		### wedding used to be invisible here on purpose: it was already inside the
		### head row's total, and two numbers that must agree are one too many
		### (item 1.2). With the head row gone the +40 has no other home in the
		### sheet, and leaving it out would put the sheet 40 points below the scale
		### whenever the promise is on. It is read from tnt_marriage_grand_value -
		### the same script value tnt_marriage_offer_p_value adds (:1200), not a
		### copy of the number - so there is still only one 40 in the mod.
		### THE DREAD ROW, V15. It exists for the same reason the +40 grand-wedding
		### row above exists: the balance now prices vanilla's whole dread ladder
		### inside the marriage term (up to 150 points either way,
		### tnt_52_marriage_values.txt section 12), and a sheet that does not print
		### it stops summing to the centre number exactly when the number is largest.
		### The identity this row closes is stated in the header of
		### tnt_marriage_dread_sheet_value (tnt_52_marriage_values.txt:3002-3011), which names this widget as its ONE
		### caller. It is the display twin, NOT the term: tnt_marriage_dread_sheet_value
		### deliberately breaks the tnt_val_<item>_<side>_value naming so the totals
		### cannot sum it a second time, and it keeps a cost NEGATIVE (`max = 0`)
		### because the sheet prints the effect on the balance.
		### HIDDEN WHEN THERE IS NO FEAR: the value returns 0 whenever neither
		### direction's dread rung fires, so the row is gated on a non-zero reading
		### rather than on the term being switched on - a row that says nothing costs
		### 26 px of the centre column, which is the argument the taper row above
		### makes for itself. The test is the mod's OWN macro, not a hand-rolled
		### datafunction chain: TntZero is defined at data_binding\tnt_macros.txt:132
		### as EqualTo_CFixedPoint( ...ScriptValue(V), '(CFixedPoint)0' ), and Not()
		### around a macro is shipped inside TntNeg itself (:127). CFixedPoint is the
		### right comparand - a script value is fixed point, and comparing it as an
		### int would round a real reading to zero.
		### NO X: like the taper, this is a derived consequence of the couples on the
		### table, not a term of its own - it is removed by removing the pair.
		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_wed_dread"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[Not( TntZero('tnt_marriage_dread_sheet_value') )]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_marriage.dds" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_item_wed_dread" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "tnt_wed_dread_detail" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_marriage_dread_sheet_value')|+=0]" }
		}

		tnt_sheet_row = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_wed_grand"
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_visible" { visible = "[And( TntOn('tnt_wed_grand'), TntVar('tnt_wed_principal').Char.IsValid )]" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_icon"    { texture = "gfx/interface/icons/character_interactions/icon_marriage.dds" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_name"    { text = "tnt_wed_grand" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_detail"  { text = "tnt_wed_grand_couple" }
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_points"  { text = "[TntSV('tnt_marriage_grand_value')|+=0]" }
			### X wired to the gate-free tnt_wed_grand_clear (tnt_22a_marriage.txt),
			### shipped in V5 exactly because the toggle's gold/DLC gates must not
			### be able to refuse a removal.
			blockoverride "tnt_sheet_remove"
			{
				button_cancel_small = {
					size = { 20 20 }
					parentanchor = center
					tooltip = "tnt_sheet_remove_tip"
					onclick = "[TntExec('tnt_wed_grand_clear')]"
				}
			}
		}

		########################################################
		### ITEM 2.2 - THE THREE MULTI-OBJECT TERMS OF THIS SIDE.
		###
		### Each is ONE instance of a group type from
		### gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui: a head row carrying the whole term's
		### price, plus a nested `datamodel` naming every chosen object under it.
		### That is the "6 nested datamodels, one per selection list" of
		### 30-V2-DESIGN section 3.4, and it is what complaint 3.4 asked for
		### literally - the selected options in the centre, IN FULL.
		###
		### THESE THREE LINES REPLACED A HAND-WRITTEN COPY, and the deletion was
		### the point. This file first grew its own aggregate row plus its own
		### 22 px `tnt_sheet_subrow`, written before gui\tnt_panel_multiselect.gui
		### existed. The two row families would have been SIBLINGS IN ONE
		### SCROLLBOX, so any divergence in height, spacing, margin or column
		### width between them shows up as a visibly ragged sheet -
		### author:multiselect's own header promises the geometry is "copied from
		### tnt_sheet_row TO THE PIXEL" (26 px, spacing 6, margin { 4 3 }, name
		### column 118, points column 52, middle column the only expanding one),
		### and that is a promise only one of two copies can keep. That author
		### also owns every identifier each group needs - tnt_count_sel_*_value,
		### tnt_val_*_multi_*_value, the six tnt_multi_count_* loc keys - none of
		### which this file could have named without guessing across an agent
		### boundary, which is exactly how build 1 shipped defect B-3.
		###
		### Order is unchanged: title, then claim, then artifact, in the same
		### places in the sheet the single-object rows occupied, so the eye still
		### tracks a term from the column it was set in down to the sheet.
		########################################################
		### V6 (user item 7): the three groups of this side NOW CARRY AN X on
		### their HEAD rows, wired inside author:multiselect's own types to the
		### gate-free tnt_sel_<item>_p_clear (tnt_22b_multiselect.txt) - see
		### the 2a box. The per-object rows below each head stay X-less (no
		### per-object sgui exists); single-object removal lives where it
		### always did, the picker panel's chosen column.
		tnt_sheet_sel_title_p = {
			name = "tnt_sheet_group_title_p"
		}

